WEBVTT

00:00:00.019 --> 00:00:02.312
We will approach SQL gradually.

00:00:02.312 --> 00:00:06.332
The first part is about reading data.

00:00:06.332 --> 00:00:11.722
You will use SELECT, choose columns, create aliases, apply filters, sort rows, and limit results.

00:00:11.722 --> 00:00:16.762
The second part is about changing data with INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE.

00:00:16.762 --> 00:00:23.582
Here we add stronger safety habits such as previewing target rows, using unique keys,

00:00:23.582 --> 00:00:25.237
and using RETURNING to see what changed.

00:00:25.237 --> 00:00:31.322
The third part is about protection and control.

00:00:31.322 --> 00:00:36.047
Transactions let us test changes and either keep them with COMMIT or undo them with ROLLBACK.

00:00:36.047 --> 00:00:38.982
The exercises follow the same progression.

00:00:38.982 --> 00:00:45.712
First run complete examples, then change one piece, then complete missing parts,

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:47.747
and finally write short statements yourself.

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:54.777
Focus on understanding the pattern before trying to memorize syntax.

00:00:54.777 --> 00:01:01.157
Connect this idea to a data engineer moving semi-structured API payloads into PostgreSQL.

00:01:01.157 --> 00:01:04.987
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:01:04.987 --> 00:01:14.277
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:01:14.277 --> 00:01:19.837
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:19.837 --> 00:01:25.501
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
